<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melchior Ndadaye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melchior Ndadaye was the first democratically elected president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> and its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> head of state. Leader of the FRODEBU party, he won the election of June 1993 and took office in July. He was killed in a coup attempt by Tutsi army officers on 21 October 1993, and the massacres that followed began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundian-civil-war">Burundian Civil War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melchior-ndadaye.jpg" alt="Melchior Ndadaye" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RTNB (Burundi National Radio and Television) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melchior_Ndadaye_speaking_to_RTNB_at_Bujumbura_airport.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Melchior Ndadaye was born on 28 March 1953 in the commune of Nyabihanga, Ruanda-Urundi, the first of ten children in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> family. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikiza">Ikiza</a> of 1972, in which the Burundian government targeted and massacred educated Hutus, he fled to Rwanda and finished his schooling at Butare. He earned a degree at the National University of Rwanda in 1980 and taught at Save until 1983.</p><p>In exile he founded a movement for Burundian Hutu students in 1976 and, in 1979, helped found the Marxist Burundi Workers' Party. He left it in 1983 over its split between armed revolution and democracy, and returned to Burundi, where he worked in banking and gained a diploma in higher banking studies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In June 1986 Ndadaye and other former members of the Workers' Party founded the underground Front for Democracy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> (FRODEBU), and he became its president. In 1988 he criticised the government of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-buyoya">Pierre Buyoya</a> and was imprisoned for two months. In 1991 he was the only opposition member of Buyoya's Constitution Commission and resigned from it in August, citing its lack of diversity.</p><p>FRODEBU was recognised on 23 July 1992 and nominated him for the presidency on 18 April 1993. He campaigned for a 'new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a>', and proposed rebuilding the Tutsi-dominated army on more equitable recruitment. 20 per cent, in an election certified as free and fair.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Ndadaye was sworn in on 10 July 1993. His government of 23 ministers included nine Tutsi, among them the Prime Minister Sylvie Kinigi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-for-national-progress">UPRONA</a>, and he named a Council of National Unity of 15 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutus</a> and 15 Tutsis. Yet he questioned contracts approved under earlier Tutsi governments and began to change the recruitment rules of the army and police, which threatened the army and the Tutsi elite. A plot by a few officers on 3 July had already failed.</p><p>On the night of 20 to 21 October 1993, armoured cars of the 11th Armoured Car Battalion surrounded the Presidential Palace, and soldiers captured the president. He was taken to the camp of the 1st Parachute Battalion and killed by junior officers. The coup soon failed, and Kinigi became acting president. The killings that followed claimed many thousands of lives, estimated at over 100,000, and grew into the decade-long Burundi Civil War. Ndadaye is remembered in Burundi as a martyr for democracy, and Ndadaye Day is observed on 21 October.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Melchior Ndadaye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1993 <b>Melchior Ndadaye</b> became the first elected Hutu president, but his assassination months later started a civil war that killed some 300,000 people.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-burundi">Republic of Burundi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Bujumbura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-3.38,29.36&amp;year=1979" aria-label="Bujumbura on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Melchior Ndadaye"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4945 5289h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4945 5289h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-3.38,29.36&amp;year=1979">What was here in 1979? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
